-- rules/inquiry/obligations.rofl — proof obligations and their status,
-- derived in the shared working book (reads [epistemic] via imports).

-- obligations are stated over canonical targets: frames may name aliases
-- (requires_on/blocks_on normalize via rules/inquiry/terminology.rofl).
obligation(I, C)           :- inquiry(I, _), requires_on(I, C).
obligation(I, C)           :- inquiry(I, _), blocks_on(I, C).

open_obligation(I, C)      :- obligation(I, C), unknown[epistemic](C).
contested_obligation(I, C) :- obligation(I, C), contested[epistemic](C).
resolved_obligation(I, C)  :- obligation(I, C), supported[epistemic](C),
                              not refuted[epistemic](C).
refuted_obligation(I, C)   :- obligation(I, C), refuted[epistemic](C),
                              not contested[epistemic](C).

-- Blocking verdicts run on the CONFIRMED grade (decision b): an
-- agent-attached polarity moves a blocking claim only after confirmation.
violated_blocking(I, C)    :- inquiry(I, _), blocks_on(I, C),
                              refuted_confirmed[epistemic](C).

-- go_blocked: what stands between this inquiry and an unconditional GO.
-- Two ways to block: a blocking claim without confirmed support, or a
-- contested one (roadmap §20: a contested blocking claim prevents GO;
-- contested is judged on the plain grade — conservative on purpose).
go_blocked(I, C)           :- inquiry(I, _), blocks_on(I, C),
                              not supported_confirmed[epistemic](C).
go_blocked(I, C)           :- inquiry(I, _), blocks_on(I, C),
                              contested[epistemic](C).

-- A GAP IS A GAP WHETHER IT IS UNKNOWN OR KNOWN FALSE, and until 2026-09-11
-- only the unknown ones were counted -- so a requirement the evidence REFUTED
-- was invisible to every verdict while an unknown one had to be accepted by
-- name, an ordering exactly inverted. Both now demand the same thing: an
-- authority who accepts them BY NAME. The claim is named in the row rather
-- than counted, so a reader is told which.
--
-- HERE RATHER THAN IN A PACK, because it is a property of an OBLIGATION and
-- not of a domain -- which is what the second decision pack made visible: the
-- rules were written once for production readiness and were about neither
-- production nor readiness.
unaccepted_gap(I, C)       :- open_obligation(I, C), not gap_accepted(I, C).
unaccepted_gap(I, C)       :- refuted_obligation(I, C), not gap_accepted(I, C).

-- What a CONDITIONAL verdict is conditional ON: the residual risk somebody
-- with authority took.
accepted_residual(I, C)    :- gap_accepted(I, C), obligation(I, C).

-- ACCEPTING WHAT IS NOT A GAP IS NOISE IN THE RECORD, not extra safety: an
-- acceptance is a decision about risk and a resolved obligation carries none.
idle_acceptance[audit](I, C) :- gap_accepted(I, C), resolved_obligation(I, C).

-- COVERAGE, over whatever concern list the activated pack declares. Generic
-- because the SHAPE is generic; the axes themselves are the pack's.
concern_covered(I, Con)    :- concern_claim(I, Con, C), claim(C).

-- A CONCERN NOBODY CAN COVER READ AS ONE NOBODY COVERED, and for a library
-- five of nine did -- `billing` scored against a language exactly as an
-- unmeasured load test would against a service. The judgement belongs where
-- the authority is, so it is asserted in the frame and subtracted here.
not_applicable(I, Con)     :- excluded_concern(I, Con, _), decision_authority(I, _).

coverage_gap(I, Con)       :- pack(I, P), concern(P, Con),
                              not concern_covered(I, Con),
                              not not_applicable(I, Con).

-- AND AN EXCLUSION THAT ALSO HAS A CLAIM IS A CONTRADICTION, not belt and
-- braces: somebody covered the axis and somebody said it could not be covered.
both_minds[audit](I, Con)  :- not_applicable(I, Con), concern_covered(I, Con).

-- preliminary verdicts; a full GO is a domain certificate (Phase 3),
-- derived per decision pack, never by a generic kernel rule.
recommendation(I, no_go)   :- violated_blocking(I, _).
